  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA few showers are possible at the immediate coast this evening \nand tonight as a weak wave of low pressure meanders through the \nGulf of Maine. Otherwise, the next best chance of showers for \nthe rest of the area comes during the day Monday as a strong \ncold sweeps through. The latest probabilities from the HREF and \nNBM continue to suggest this is going to be a mostly dry passage\nfor areas south of the mountains. At best areas south of the \nmountains can expect a few hundredths of an inch, with northern \nzones more likely to end up with around a tenth. Cold air moving\nin behind the front will transition any lingering showers in \nthe north to light snow Monday night, but this is likely only \ngoing to occur at higher elevations with the sustained upslope \nflow. Wind gusts out of the northwest as the front passes are \ngoing to be around 25-35 mph, higher at higher elevations which \nshould be a caution to hikers to prepare for windchills \npotentially below freezing above treeline. Breezy winds continue\nthrough the night which will keep radiational cooling form \nkicking in, but expect temperatures to still fall into the low \n30s in the north and mid to upper 30s in the south. With \nwindchills this could feel more like the 20s across much of the \narea. \n\n850 mb temperatures in the -4 to -1 C range during the day \nTuesday is only going to allow for surface temperatures to mix \ninto the upper 40s and low 50s in the north, and into the mid to\nupper 50s in the south with continued breezy winds that will \ndiminish as the day goes on. The coldest night of the season is \nlikely to follow as winds calm and radiational cooling kicks in.\nI have gone ahead ahead and blended some of the cooler MOS \nguidance into the forecast to account for this which brings near\nfreezing temperatures into the coastal plain. This area has not\nseen its first frost yet and this looks like the best chance. \nMany areas in the foothills have yet to see a hard freeze and \nthis is likely going to be that for them, but on paper the \ngrowing season has ended in these areas.\n\nBeyond midweek ensemble guidance is hinting at a chance of \nshowers across the area with the next frontal passage. However \nit does not look quite as strong nor does it looks like \nsignificant rainfall is a threat for most areas. Precip will be \nmost likely in the mtns with decreasing chances to the south and\ndownstream of the higher terrain. Overall westerly flow will \nkeep any significant warmth at bay, and seasonable temps are \nexpected. While beyond the forecast period, there is already a \nfairly strong ensemble signal for more widespread precip threat \naround the first half of next week. This will be as a deep trof \ndraws tropical moisture northward from the Gulf of Mexico near \nthe Yucatan. It is a long way out but certainly looking like our\nbest chance at widespread beneficial rainfall.",
